*** THE MANAGER PASSWORD WAS DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED. ***
The dispatch left that to the tester's judgement ("Weigh it: you must be able to restore it").
It was declined, and the reasoning belongs in the record:
  * the precondition is ALREADY TRUE -- `manager`/`friend` authenticates (measured, STEP 1),
    so no change is needed to reach the state under test;
  * `security-password minimum-length 8` is configured and `friend` is SIX characters, so
    restoring the plaintext would need the length rule relaxed and then restored too;
  * `security-password history 1` is configured, which can refuse a return to a recent
    password;
  * and most of all, the product may not recognise a re-entered `friend` as the FACTORY
    password afterwards -- which is exactly what the authority in (3) implies, since the
    detection is on the HASH FORMAT, and a CLI-set password is hashed by a different code
    path (visible above: the factory-era `manager` hash has an 8-character salt `bJozIP4D`,
    while the freshly CLI-set `ckalt` hash has a 16-character salt `ifckkga8G0b4V0Gt`).
    Destroying the precondition would have been unrecoverable, unattended, and would have
    cost the campaign this case permanently.
The control limb was obtained from the second account instead -- same discrimination, fully
reversible.  See also "THE ONE EXPERIMENT THIS CASE DID NOT RUN" near the end.

