=================================================================================
DESIGNED PROCEDURE
=================================================================================
The question is a single one -- does the DUT print `% Default password needs to be changed.`
while the `manager` account still has the factory password `friend`?  It cannot be answered
by any `show` command, because the TFS specifies no show and no log; it exists only in the
bytes a real login receives.  So the design is: exercise EVERY access path the DUT offers,
read the raw login byte stream on each, and -- crucially -- PROVE THE INSTRUMENT CAN SEE A
MESSAGE AT THAT EXACT POINT, so that an absence is a measurement and not a blind spot.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 -- PRECONDITION: the manager account is still on the factory password
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Designed expectation: `manager` / `friend` authenticates.  If it does not, the case has no
precondition and nothing after it means anything.

    tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckmgmt u2 115200 --user manager --password friend --logout
        --settle 30
    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4  prompt='awplus>'
    conlogin: logged out again
    conlogin: restore login as manager -> USER ('awplus>')   (exit 0)

`friend` authenticates.  Landing at `awplus>` rather than `awplus#` is correct for AW+ even
for a `privilege 15` username (platforms/x960.md §7E.6).
STEP 1 VERDICT: PASS -- the precondition holds, no password change was needed.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 -- ACCESS PATH 1 of 3: the CONSOLE.  No warning.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Designed expectation (TFS 2616.1 example): after the `AlliedWare Plus (TM)` banner and before
`awplus>`, the line `% Default password needs to be changed.`

The byte stream, read from the console transcript /tmp/ckmgmt/console-u2.log (conlogin prints
only a one-line summary; the transcript holds every byte).  TWO consecutive logins, VERBATIM:

    awplus#exit

    awplus login: manager
    Password:
    Last login: Mon Oct 19 22:48:24 UTC 2026 from 10.38.22.33
    AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

    awplus>exit

    awplus login:
    awplus login: manager
    Password:
    Last login: Mon Oct 19 22:58:09 UTC 2026 on ttyS0
    AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/12/26 06:38:00

    awplus>enable
    awplus#

*** NO `% Default password needs to be changed.` LINE, on either login. ***
The banner is `Last login:` then the version line then the prompt, with nothing between the
version line and the prompt -- which is exactly where the TFS example puts the warning.
(The `awplus#end  ^ % Invalid input detected` immediately before this is the driver sending
`end` at an exec prompt; the dispatch flagged it as the tester's artefact, not a product
fault, and it is not graded.)
STEP 2 VERDICT: the warning is ABSENT on the console.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 -- ACCESS PATHS 2 and 3: TELNET and SSH.  No warning on either.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Designed expectation: the same warning, since 2616.1 says "at every login using the default
account 'manager'" and names no access path.  Telnet was the dispatch's main suspect, because
it is the one path the console had not covered.

TELNET (work/v-telnet-manager.log), VERBATIM:

