---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 8 -- WHY: the product's own documented detection cannot fire on this DUT
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This step is analysis against the authority, not a new measurement, and is labelled as such.

The current FDPC engineering page says, verbatim:
    "Currently, we only check that the password has been encrypted with an MD5 hash, as the
     default-generated config contains this but a user-generated config will not."

The DUT's stored password, measured:
    username manager privilege 15 password 8 $5$bJozIP4D$orvkW78S6QHN65L3EGGJpHS.QlHSjwuYbMynH5.Ifp2

`$5$` is the crypt(3) prefix for **SHA-256-crypt**.  MD5-crypt is `$1$`.  swi_b is the same:
`$5$bJom.f4D$KWOF...`.  So on both units the manager password is stored as SHA-256, and the
documented detection -- which looks for an MD5 hash -- cannot match, even though the password
literally IS `friend` and authenticates as such.

Two supporting observations, both measured:
  * `flash:/default.cfg`, the DUT's boot config (1049 bytes, dated Oct 16 2026 06:22:58),
    begins `! Startup-config saved on Wed Mar 22 17:15:11 2000 +0000 by manager via con0
    (ttyS0)` and contains `service password-encryption` and the same `$5$bJozIP4D$...` line
    (work/v-defaultcfg.log).  So it is a SAVED startup config, not a pristine factory file --
    which is relevant, because the engineering page's own caveat is that the heuristic depends
    on the config having come from the factory generator.  It is therefore NOT established
    from the bench whether the `$5$` form arrived from the factory or from a
    `copy running-config startup-config` performed while the unit still had `friend`.  Either
    way the user-visible outcome is the same, and it is the outcome the TPS row promises.
  * The hash SALT LENGTHS differ by origin: both units' `manager` hashes carry an
    8-character salt (`bJozIP4D`, `bJom.f4D`), while the password this case set through the
    CLI (`ckalt`) got a 16-character salt (`ifckkga8G0b4V0Gt`).  Two different generators, so
    the `manager` hashes were not produced by an ordinary CLI `username ... password` command.
  * No `default-password` CLI is exposed: `default-password ?` and `show default-password ?`
    both answer `% Unrecognized command` (work/v-qmark-dp.log).  The internal IMISH commands
    named in ER361 are not reachable from a user session, so the state cannot be queried.

=> The absence is consistent, end to end, with a detection that keys on the MD5 hash FORMAT
rather than on the password VALUE, on a build whose stored hash is SHA-256.
STEP 8 VERDICT: the mechanism is identified from the product's own documentation.

=================================================================================
NOT MEASURED / NOT MEASURABLE, SAID PLAINLY
=================================================================================
* **The `def_pwd_chg` boot variable was NOT read.**  The FDPC override lives in the U-Boot
  environment (`setenv relargs def_pwd_chg=<value>`), there is no CLI that reports it, and
  reaching the bootloader needs a reboot.  THIS CAMPAIGN PERFORMS NO RELOAD (the underlay is
  running-config only and the boot config is `flash:/default.cfg`, so a reload destroys the
  measurement path for every case).  So it is not proven that the warning is not simply
  switched off on this unit.  AGAINST that reading: the case's own feature, TFS 2616, is the
  warn-only successor and names no gate at all, its "Feature functionality that will not be
  implemented" is None and its Excluded Targets is None, and the TPS row for the x960 says
  Yes.  FOR it: 2616 may well have been implemented on FDPC's gated code path.  This is the
  single open question in the case and it is flagged to the Test Engineer.
* **HTTP login is NOT MEASURABLE on this DUT** -- no Web GUI file is installed (`GUI file in
  use : -`) and installing one is a bench change.  The login-only-mode API behaviour WAS
  measured instead and is reported above.
* **REBOOT PERSISTENCE IS NOT MEASURED**, for this case and the whole group, for the reason
  just given.  Same declared gap as groups A and B.
* The IPv6 management path was not used (no IPv6 address on the DUT's SVIs).
* No attempt was made to make the warning appear by changing the manager password -- see the
  next section.

THE ONE EXPERIMENT THIS CASE DID NOT RUN, AND EXACTLY HOW TO RUN IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 8's conclusion is drawn from the product's documentation plus the measured hash format;
it is not measured on hardware.  ONE experiment would measure it directly:

    set the manager password to an MD5-crypt ($1$) hash of 'friend', then log in and look.

    show running-config | include username manager      <- record the EXACT line first
    configure terminal
    username manager privilege 15 password 8 $1$<salt>$<md5crypt of 'friend'>
    end
    (then a console/telnet login as manager/friend, and look for the warning)
    restore:  username manager privilege 15 password 8 \
              $5$bJozIP4D$orvkW78S6QHN65L3EGGJpHS.QlHSjwuYbMynH5.Ifp2

If the warning appears under `$1$` and not under `$5$`, the MD5-format theory is proven on
hardware and the finding becomes airtight.

IT WAS NOT RUN, DELIBERATELY, AND THE REASON IS NOT TIMIDITY:
  * it touches the DUT's ONLY administrative account, on an UNATTENDED run;
  * `security-password history 1` is configured and may refuse the restoring command, which
    would leave the unit on an MD5 hash of `friend` with no way back from this seat;
  * if the device then decides it IS in the factory-default state, ER361's behaviour is
    FORCE_CHANGE, not WARN -- the login would demand an interactive new password, and
    `conlogin.py` by design refuses a forced password-change dialogue rather than guessing,
    which could leave a console mid-dialogue;
  * swi_a on this very bench is already unusable this campaign because of a login lockout,
    and a second lost unit would be a serious bench incident.
It needs an attended session.  It is reported to the sentinel as a NEEDS TEST ENGINEER item
with these exact commands.

