RAISED TO THE TEST ENGINEER (recorded here, sent to the sentinel, not blocking)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. *** PRODUCT FINDING -- a device still using the factory password `friend` gives the user NO
   warning on ANY access path, on two x960 units and two builds, although TPS row 271 says
   Yes and TFS 2616.1 says it must happen at every `manager` login.  The product's own
   engineering page gives the mechanism: the detection "only check[s] that the password has
   been encrypted with an MD5 hash", and this build stores it as SHA-256 (`$5$`).  The DUT's
   HTTP subsystem agrees it is not in the factory-default state (RestCONF answered 200, not
   the documented 403 login-only-mode refusal).  This wants a developer.
2. The ONE open question that would close it: is the `def_pwd_chg` boot variable simply unset
   on these units, and was TFS 2616 implemented on FDPC's gated path?  Not readable without a
   reboot, which this campaign forbids.
3. The one experiment not run (MD5 hash substitution on the `manager` account) and why -- the
   commands and the exact restore string are above; it needs an attended session.
4. VERDICT CONFIRMATION REQUESTED: this is graded FAIL (the feature is declared supported and
   the behaviour is absent).  If the Test Engineer judges that `def_pwd_chg` being unset makes
   the silence correct, the grade becomes UNSUPPORTED-by-configuration or PARTIAL.  The
   evidence supports either reading and the choice is the Test Engineer's.

RUN LABEL: clean.
VERDICT: FAIL -- the warning `% Default password needs to be changed.` was absent from EVERY
login path on a DUT whose `manager` account still authenticates with the factory password
`friend`: two console logins, telnet, SSH, and two console logins on a second x960 of a
different model and build in the same state.  The instruments were proven able to show a
message at exactly that point in the login (a `banner exec` marker appeared on all three
paths), so the absence is a measurement and not a blind spot; the DUT's HTTP subsystem
independently reported that it does not consider itself to be in the factory-default-password
state (RestCONF 200 OK where login-only mode specifies 403); no log line was produced either.
TPS row 271 for the x960 reads Yes and TFS 2616.1 requires the warning at every `manager`
login with no configuration, so this is a FINDING rather than a platform limit, and the
product's own FDPC engineering page supplies the likely cause: detection keys on an MD5 (`$1$`)
password hash while this build stores SHA-256 (`$5$`).  One limb is NOT MEASURED -- the
`def_pwd_chg` boot variable, unreadable without a reboot this campaign forbids -- and the
grade is flagged to the Test Engineer for confirmation.
